.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt TERM-TITLE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm term-title
.Nd set the terminal's title, or run a command under its own
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl b | Fl i | Fl w
.Op Ar text ...
.Nm
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl b | Fl i | Fl w
.Fl e Ar command Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl T Ar type
.Op Fl b | Fl i | Fl w
.Fl r | Fl s
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sets the window title of a terminal, its icon name, or both.
The text is the arguments joined by spaces; none clears it.
Control characters are removed from it, so a title taken from a file or
a host name cannot slip an escape sequence to the terminal.
.Pp
With
.Fl e ,
the title is the command line: the previous title is saved on the
terminal's title stack, the command is run under the new one, and the
previous one is restored when the command ends, on Ctrl-C too.
The title is restored from the terminal's own stack rather than from a
title read back, because reading a title back is a query most terminals
refuse to answer.
.Pp
The sequences sent follow
.Ev TERM ,
or
.Fl T :
.Bl -tag -width "screen"
.It Cm xterm
xterm, and every terminal speaking its OSC 0, 1 and 2 sequences
.Pq rxvt, alacritty, foot, kitty, st, wezterm, ghostty, those based on VTE or Konsole... :
the window title, the icon name, which several show as the tab title,
or both; and the title stack, for
.Fl s ,
.Fl r
and
.Fl e .
.It Cm screen
.Xr screen 1
and
.Xr tmux 1 :
the window name, whatever
.Fl w ,
.Fl i
or
.Fl b
say.
They keep no title stack, so
.Fl s
and
.Fl r
are refused, and
.Fl e
leaves the command's name in place when it ends.
.El
.Pp
A terminal whose
.Ev TERM
is in neither family is refused, unless
.Fl T
names the sequences to send; with
.Fl e ,
the command is run all the same, untitled.
.Pp
The sequences go to the terminal,
.Pa /dev/tty ,
not to stdout, so a command whose output is redirected
.Pq Ql term-title -e make > build.log
never puts escape codes into the file.
Where there is no terminal, they go to stdout, and
.Fl o
names a file outright.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-o file"
.It Fl b
Set both the window title and the icon name; the default.
.It Fl e Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Run
.Ar command
with its command line as the title, then restore the title it had.
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl i
Set the icon name, the tab title in several terminals.
.It Fl o Ar file
Append the sequences to
.Ar file
instead of writing them to the terminal.
.It Fl q
Quiet: say nothing about a terminal that cannot be titled.
.It Fl r
Restore the title last saved on the terminal's stack.
.It Fl s
Save the title on the terminal's stack.
.It Fl T Ar type
Send
.Cm xterm
or
.Cm screen
sequences, whatever
.Ev TERM
says.
.It Fl w
Set the window title.
.El
.Pp
.Fl b , i
and
.Fl w
exclude one another, and so do
.Fl e , r
and
.Fl s ;
with
.Fl r
or
.Fl s ,
the one chosen says which title the stack saves or restores.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TERM
.It Ev TERM
The terminal's type, which decides the sequences sent, unless
.Fl T .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The title was set, saved or restored.
.It 1
A terminal that cannot be titled, or
.Fl s
or
.Fl r
under
.Xr screen 1
or
.Xr tmux 1 .
.It 2
A usage error.
.El
.Pp
With
.Fl e ,
the exit status is the command's: 130, 143 or 129 when
.Nm
is interrupted, terminated or hung up while it runs.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Name the window after the host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ term-title "$(uname -n)"
.Ed
.Pp
A long build under its own title, the old one back afterwards:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ term-title -e make -j8
.Ed
.Pp
Only the tab title:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ term-title -i logs
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr screen 1 ,
.Xr tmux 1 ,
.Xr xterm 1
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
knows terminals by
.Ev TERM ;
one outside its list is refused unless
.Fl T
names the sequences to send.
Restoring a title needs the terminal's title stack, which
.Xr screen 1
and
.Xr tmux 1
lack.
